{"topic":"trust","summary":"how to verify a tau release is authentic — checksums, cosign/Sigstore signatures, SLSA provenance attestations, and CycloneDX SBOMs","details":"Releases produced by the signing pipeline ship a layered trust story — pick the level you need; each layer is independent and strictly stronger than the last. Layer 1, checksum: every release publishes SHA256SUMS.txt covering all archives, and install.sh/install.ps1 verify it fail-closed before writing the binary (TAU_SKIP_CHECKSUM=1 opts out — only with another trust path; manual check: grep ' <asset>$' SHA256SUMS.txt | sha256sum -c -). Layer 2, signature: opt-in cosign/Sigstore verification of the <asset>.sigstore.json bundle — `tau upgrade --verify-signature` or `TAU_VERIFY_SIGSTORE=1` for install.sh/install.ps1; it requires cosign on PATH and fails closed when cosign or the bundle is missing. Manual check: cosign verify-blob --bundle <asset>.sigstore.json --certificate-identity-regexp '^https://github\\.com/javimosch/tau/\\.github/workflows/release\\.yml@refs/tags/v' --certificate-oidc-issuer 'https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com' <asset>. All signing is keyless — the release job mints a short-lived Fulcio certificate from its GitHub OIDC identity and records every signature in the Rekor transparency log, so there is no private key to lose or rotate. Layer 3, provenance: SLSA v1 build-provenance attestations for every asset live in the repo's GitHub attestation store — gh attestation verify <asset> --repo javimosch/tau proves which repo, workflow, ref, and runner produced the bytes (no bundle download needed). Layer 4, contents: <asset>.sbom.json is a CycloneDX SBOM — the first component carries the archive's own SHA-256 and the rest hash every file inside the archive; SBOMs are signed and attested like the archives. Caveats: signature bundles, attestations, and SBOMs only exist on releases produced after the signing pipeline shipped — v0.4.0 predates it, so opt-in verification fails closed there (a missing bundle is the intended signal, not an error to bypass); a failed cosign verify-blob or gh attestation verify means the asset was not produced by the release workflow — do not run it.","see_also":["tau upgrade --verify-signature","tau version --check","tau explain TAU_VERIFY_SIGSTORE","tau explain TAU_SKIP_CHECKSUM","tau explain TAU_BASE_URL","tau explain privacy"],"docs":"https://github.com/javimosch/tau/blob/master/docs/troubleshooting.md#install--upgrade-failures"}
